ch = p.chapter("physical", "Physical geography: coasts, glaciation and hydrology")
drift_fig = shapes([
    LINE(20, 130, 420, 130, width=4), T(30, 150, "Beach", 12, anchor="start"), T(30, 40, "Sea", 12, anchor="start"),
    LINE(60, 70, 110, 125, color="red", width=2, arrow="end"), LINE(110, 125, 110, 70, color="blue", width=2, arrow="end"),
    LINE(110, 70, 160, 125, color="red", width=2, arrow="end"), LINE(160, 125, 160, 70, color="blue", width=2, arrow="end"),
    LINE(160, 70, 210, 125, color="red", width=2, arrow="end"), LINE(210, 125, 210, 70, color="blue", width=2, arrow="end"),
    T(290, 70, "Swash: oblique", 12, anchor="start", color="red"), T(290, 95, "Backwash: straight back", 12, anchor="start", color="blue"),
    LINE(60, 200, 330, 200, width=3, arrow="end"), T(195, 190, "Net movement of sand and shingle", 12),
], 440, 220)
L = lesson(ch, "coasts", "Coastal processes, landforms and management",
    ["Distinguish constructive and destructive waves", "Explain erosion, longshore drift and the formation of coastal landforms", "Evaluate hard and soft coastal management, including mangroves"],
    [("definition", "Waves and erosion", "**Constructive waves** are low and long, with a strong swash and weak backwash: they build beaches. **Destructive waves** are high and steep, with a weak swash and strong backwash: they remove material. Erosion processes: **hydraulic action**, **abrasion**, **attrition**, **solution**. Weathering and mass movement also shape cliffs."),
     ("retenir", "Landforms", "**Erosion:** cliffs with a wave-cut notch and platform; headlands, caves, arches and stacks. **Deposition:** beaches, spits (a ridge of sand or shingle attached to land at one end), bars and tombolos, formed where **longshore drift** carries material along the coast, driven by waves arriving at an angle. Rivers deliver sediment to deltas, estuaries and mangrove coasts."),
     ("retenir", "Management", "**Hard engineering:** sea walls, groynes, rip-rap: effective locally but costly and can starve other beaches. **Soft engineering:** beach nourishment, dune restoration, mangrove protection and replanting. **Managed retreat** lets the sea reclaim land. Mangroves line parts of the Cameroonian coast and estuaries; they protect shores, shelter fish nurseries and supply wood, but they are cut for fuel and building."),
     ("pieges", "Common slips", "- Swash is the wave running up the beach; backwash is the return flow.\n- Longshore drift is a movement along the coast, not onto it.\n- Hard engineering protects one place but may increase erosion downdrift.\n- Do not describe a stack as a cave: the order is cave, arch, stack, stump.")],
    [("Example 1 — Spit formation", "Explain briefly how a spit forms.",
      ["Waves arriving at an angle move sand along the beach by longshore drift.", "Where the coastline changes direction, for example at a river mouth, the material continues in a straight line into deeper water.", "Deposition builds a ridge attached to land at one end.", "Wave refraction and wind may curve the end."], "Longshore drift deposits material where the coast bends, building a ridge attached at one end."),
     ("Example 2 — Management choice", "A fishing village on a sandy coast is losing land. Suggest and justify one management approach.",
      ["Option: protect or restore mangroves and dunes, with groynes only if needed.", "Mangroves absorb wave energy, trap sediment and support fisheries, at low cost.", "A concrete wall would be costly and could shift erosion to neighbouring beaches."], "Soft approach: restore mangroves/dunes for cheap, sustainable protection.")],
    [M("Which waves build up beaches?", "Constructive waves", ["Destructive waves", "Tsunamis only", "Waves with a strong backwash"], "Strong swash and weak backwash deposit material."),
     TF("Longshore drift moves sediment along a coast.", True, "It is movement parallel to the shore."),
     MT("Match each landform to its process.", [("Wave-cut platform", "Erosion at the cliff base"), ("Spit", "Deposition by longshore drift"), ("Stack", "Erosion of a headland"), ("Mangrove", "Plants stabilising muddy coast")], "Standard landform-process links."),
     M("Which is a soft engineering method?", "Beach nourishment", ["Sea wall", "Rock armour", "Groyne of concrete"], "Soft methods work with natural processes."),
     O("Explain why a sea wall can reduce erosion at one place but cause problems elsewhere.", "A sea wall reflects wave energy and stops cliffs or land retreating where it stands. But it prevents the supply of sediment from the cliff and may bounce waves downward, scouring the beach in front. Down-drift areas lose sand and erode faster.", "Protects locally (1); blocks sediment or increases scour (1); down-drift effect (1); clear explanation (1)."),
     O("Describe the formation of caves, arches and stacks and evaluate two methods of managing a retreating coastline.", "Waves attack cracks in a headland by hydraulic action and abrasion, forming a cave. The cave erodes through the headland to form an arch. The roof collapses leaving a stack, which later becomes a stump. Management: sea walls give strong local protection but are expensive; mangrove and dune restoration is cheaper and sustainable but slower and less certain against severe storms.", "Sequence of landforms with process (2); two methods with evaluation (2).")],
    [("Destructive waves are", "High with a strong backwash", ["Low with a strong swash", "Always calm", "Only found in rivers"], "They erode beaches."),
     ("A tombolo links", "An island to the mainland by a bar", ["Two rivers", "Two lakes", "A cave and an arch"], "A deposition ridge joins them."),
     ("Solution erosion at the coast is", "Dissolving of rock such as limestone", ["Rolling of pebbles", "Scraping by load", "Freezing"], "Chemical process."),
     ("Groynes are built to", "Trap sand moving along a beach", ["Stop the wind", "Dry fish", "Produce power"], "They interrupt longshore drift."),
     ("Mangroves help coasts by", "Absorbing wave energy and trapping sediment", ["Increasing erosion", "Adding salt", "Reflecting waves"], "Their roots protect shores.")],
    (drift_fig, "Longshore drift moves sand in a zigzag along the beach.", "Zigzag arrows: swash runs up at an angle and backwash returns straight, moving sand along the beach."),
    ["Statements on mangroves along Cameroon’s coast are general: confirm local examples and conservation status.", "Spit/tombolo definitions follow common textbooks."])

glac_fig = flow(["Snow falls and builds up", "Compaction forms firn then glacial ice", "Ice flows downhill under gravity", "Plucking and abrasion erode the valley"], 440, "blue", 13, 340)
L = lesson(ch, "glaciation", "Glaciation: an introduction",
    ["Explain how glaciers form and move", "Describe glacial erosion and deposition landforms", "Place glaciation in an African and a global context"],
    [("definition", "Glaciers", "A **glacier** is a large body of ice that moves slowly under gravity. It forms where more snow falls than melts: snow is compressed into **firn** and then ice. During the ice ages of the Pleistocene, ice sheets covered large parts of the northern hemisphere; the last glacial maximum was roughly 20 000 years ago."),
     ("retenir", "Erosion and landforms", "Glaciers erode by **plucking** (tearing blocks from rock) and **abrasion** (scraping by rock debris). Landforms: **corries** (armchair hollows), **arêtes** (sharp ridges between corries), **pyramidal peaks**, **U-shaped valleys**, **hanging valleys** and **ribbon lakes**."),
     ("retenir", "Deposition", "Melting ice drops **till**, forming **moraines** (lateral, medial, terminal), **drumlins** and scattered **erratics** (rocks from far away). Meltwater forms outwash plains and eskers."),
     ("attention", "Glaciers in Africa", "Tropical Africa has no large ice sheets. A few small glaciers exist on the highest mountains of East Africa and have been shrinking; they are not found in Cameroon. Questions on glaciation usually use northern European, Alpine or Himalayan examples.")],
    [("Example 1 — Form of a U-shaped valley", "Explain how a U-shaped valley forms.",
      ["A river valley is V-shaped before glaciation.", "Ice fills the valley and erodes the floor and sides by plucking and abrasion.", "The valley becomes wider and deeper, with steep sides and a flat floor.", "After the ice melts the typical U-shaped cross-section remains."], "Glacial plucking and abrasion widen and deepen a V-valley into a U."),
     ("Example 2 — Corries and arêtes", "Explain how an arête forms.",
      ["Two corries develop on opposite sides of a mountain.", "Erosion by plucking and abrasion cuts back the back walls.", "The land between them is narrowed to a steep, knife-edge ridge: the arête."], "Back-to-back corries leave a narrow ridge.")],
    [M("What do glaciers erode by plucking?", "Blocks of rock frozen to the ice", ["Only soil", "Ocean waves", "Wind"], "Ice freezes around rock and tears it away."),
     TF("Cameroon has large glaciers on its mountains.", False, "There are no glaciers in Cameroon."),
     MT("Match each landform to its description.", [("Corrie", "Armchair-shaped hollow"), ("Arête", "Sharp ridge"), ("Erratic", "Boulder carried far from its source"), ("Terminal moraine", "Ridge at the furthest advance of the ice")], "Standard landforms."),
     M("Firn is", "Compacted snow on the way to becoming ice", ["Liquid water", "A rock type", "A type of cloud"], "It is an intermediate stage."),
     O("Describe the difference between plucking and abrasion.", "Plucking happens when meltwater freezes onto rock; as the glacier moves it pulls blocks away. Abrasion is the scraping and polishing of the bed and walls by rock fragments embedded in the ice.", "Plucking (2); abrasion (2); maximum 3."),
     O("With the help of diagrams, describe how a glacier changes a river valley and what features remain after the ice has melted.", "Before glaciation the river valley is V-shaped with interlocking spurs. A glacier fills it, straightening and deepening it by abrasion and plucking, truncating spurs and widening the sides into a U-shape. Tributary valleys are left hanging, with waterfalls. Ribbon lakes fill over-deepened parts, and moraines mark ice limits.", "U-shape explained (1); truncated spurs (1); hanging valleys (1); lakes and moraines (1).")],
    [("The last glacial maximum was approximately", "20 000 years ago", ["200 years ago", "2 million years ago", "Last century"], "Roughly 20 000 years ago."),
     ("A hanging valley is", "A tributary valley left high above the main valley", ["A frozen lake", "A river delta", "A coral reef"], "The main glacier cut deeper."),
     ("Drumlins are", "Egg-shaped hills of till", ["Sharp ridges", "Deep lakes", "Volcano cones"], "They form under moving ice."),
     ("Glacier ice moves because of", "Gravity", ["Wind", "Tides", "Waves"], "Ice flows downhill slowly."),
     ("Which is a depositional landform?", "Moraine", ["Corrie", "Arête", "U-shaped valley"], "Moraines are made of deposited till.")],
    (glac_fig, "From snow to a valley glacier that erodes.", "Four boxes connected by arrows: snow, firn and ice, ice flow, erosion by plucking and abrasion."),
    ["Date of the last glacial maximum is approximate; check the syllabus wording.", "Presence of glaciers in East Africa is stated in general terms."])

hyd_fig = flow(["Evaporation and transpiration", "Condensation: clouds form", "Precipitation", "Interception, infiltration, run-off", "Rivers and groundwater return to the sea"], 440, "blue", 13, 340)
L = lesson(ch, "hydrology", "Hydrological cycle, water balance and water resources",
    ["Describe stores and flows of the hydrological cycle", "Use the water-balance equation", "Discuss water resources and management in Africa"],
    [("definition", "Stores and flows", "**Stores:** oceans, atmosphere, ice, groundwater, rivers, soil moisture, vegetation. **Flows:** evaporation, **transpiration**, condensation, precipitation, **interception**, **infiltration**, percolation, **throughflow**, surface run-off, groundwater flow. A **drainage basin** is the area drained by a river and its tributaries, bounded by a watershed."),
     ("formule", "Water balance", "$P = Q + E \\pm \\Delta S$, where P is precipitation, Q is run-off (streamflow), E is evapotranspiration and ΔS is the change in storage. If the soil is not storing or losing water, $Q = P - E$.", "P = Q + E \\pm \\Delta S"),
     ("retenir", "Water resources", "Groundwater held in **aquifers** reaches the surface through wells and springs; the top of the saturated zone is the **water table**. In Africa, rainfall is seasonal and unreliable in many places. Dams (such as Lagdo on the Benue) store water for power and irrigation; boreholes and rainwater harvesting provide drinking water; poor sanitation spreads water-borne disease."),
     ("pieges", "Be careful", "- Infiltration is water entering the soil; percolation is deeper movement; throughflow is sideways flow in the soil.\n- Transpiration is water loss by plants; evaporation from surfaces; together evapotranspiration.\n- Dams bring benefits but also costs: displacement of people, silting and changes downstream.")],
    [("Example 1 — Water balance", "A basin receives 1500 mm of rain a year and loses 700 mm by evapotranspiration. Assuming no change in storage, find run-off.",
      ["Q = P − E.", "Q = 1500 − 700 = 800 mm."], "800 mm."),
     ("Example 2 — With storage", "In a dry year P = 900 mm, E = 700 mm and the soil and groundwater store 50 mm less water at the end of the year (ΔS = −50). Find Q.",
      ["Q = P − E − ΔS = 900 − 700 − (−50).", "Q = 200 + 50 = 250 mm: stored water has fed the river."], "250 mm.")],
    [N("P = 1200 mm, E = 800 mm, no storage change. Find Q in mm.", 400, "Q = 1200 − 800 = 400 mm.", 0, "mm"),
     M("Which term describes plants releasing water vapour?", "Transpiration", ["Infiltration", "Percolation", "Interception"], "Plants lose water through leaves."),
     TF("The watershed is the boundary of a drainage basin.", True, "Water on either side drains to different basins."),
     N("P = 1000, E = 650, ΔS = +30 (storage rises). Find Q in mm.", 320, "Q = 1000 − 650 − 30 = 320 mm.", 0, "mm"),
     M("An aquifer is", "A rock layer that stores and transmits groundwater", ["A kind of cloud", "A type of delta", "A river source"], "Aquifers hold water in pores and cracks."),
     O("Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of building a large dam on an African river.", "Advantages: stored water for irrigation and towns, hydroelectricity, flood control, fishing. Disadvantages: people and farmland are flooded, silt is trapped so downstream soils lose fertility, water-borne diseases can spread, ecosystems and fisheries downstream change, and cost is high. The balance depends on planning and compensation.", "Advantages (2); disadvantages (2); a reasoned judgement (1).")],
    [("Interception is", "Water caught by vegetation before reaching the ground", ["Water flowing in a river", "Water in the ocean", "Water in the air"], "Leaves hold rain."),
     ("Throughflow is", "Sideways movement of water through soil", ["Rain falling", "Flow in rivers", "Evaporation"], "Throughflow is slower than surface run-off."),
     ("The water table is", "The top of the saturated zone", ["The surface of a lake", "A kitchen table", "An ice layer"], "Below it all pores are full."),
     ("If P = 800 and E = 500 with no storage change, Q =", "300 mm", ["1300 mm", "500 mm", "400 mm"], "800 − 500 = 300."),
     ("A common benefit of dams is", "Hydroelectric power", ["More floods", "Lower rainfall", "Silt for rivers"], "Dams also provide irrigation water.")],
    (hyd_fig, "The hydrological cycle.", "Five boxes with arrows from evaporation to condensation, precipitation, ground processes and the return to the sea."),
    ["Check whether the syllabus uses the equation P = Q + E ± ΔS or the form P = Q + E.", "The dam example is named in general terms only."])

# ================================================================ 2 ENVIRONMENT
ch = p.chapter("environment", "Environmental management and climate change")
cc_fig = flow(["Fossil fuels, deforestation, farming", "More greenhouse gases in the air", "More heat trapped: warming", "Rainfall changes, sea-level rise, extreme events"], 440, "red", 13, 340)
L = lesson(ch, "climate-change", "Climate change: causes, impacts and responses",
    ["Explain the greenhouse effect and the enhanced greenhouse effect", "Describe impacts of climate change in Africa", "Distinguish mitigation and adaptation and name international agreements"],
    [("definition", "Greenhouse effect", "Gases such as **water vapour, carbon dioxide (CO₂), methane (CH₄) and nitrous oxide (N₂O)** trap heat radiated from the Earth. This **natural** greenhouse effect keeps the planet warm enough for life. The **enhanced** greenhouse effect results from extra gases released by burning fossil fuels, clearing forests and some farming (rice paddies, livestock)."),
     ("retenir", "Impacts in Africa", "Possible impacts vary by region: changing and less reliable rainfall, more frequent droughts and floods, heat stress, spread of some diseases, lower crop yields, pressure on water, rising sea level threatening coasts and deltas, and loss of biodiversity. Africa has contributed little to global emissions but is vulnerable because many people depend on rain-fed farming."),
     ("methode", "Responses", "**Mitigation** reduces emissions or removes carbon: renewable energy, efficient stoves, protecting forests, tree planting. **Adaptation** copes with change: drought-tolerant crops, water storage, early-warning systems, flood defences, diversifying livelihoods. International action includes the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change (1992), the Kyoto Protocol (1997) and the Paris Agreement (2015)."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- The ozone hole is a different problem from global warming.\n- Climate is long-term average conditions; weather is short-term.\n- A single hot year or one storm does not prove climate change.\n- Mitigation and adaptation are not the same thing.")],
    [("Example 1 — Classify", "Classify each as mitigation or adaptation: (a) planting forest, (b) building raised houses on a flood-prone coast, (c) installing solar panels.",
      ["(a) Forests absorb CO₂: mitigation.", "(b) Raised houses cope with flooding: adaptation.", "(c) Solar replaces fossil fuel: mitigation."], "(a) mitigation, (b) adaptation, (c) mitigation."),
     ("Example 2 — Explain a link", "Explain how deforestation contributes to climate change.",
      ["Trees store carbon as they grow.", "Burning or rotting released CO₂ to the air.", "Fewer trees remain to absorb CO₂, so more stays in the atmosphere and enhances warming."], "It releases stored carbon and removes a carbon sink.")],
    [M("Which gas is the main greenhouse gas released by burning fossil fuels?", "Carbon dioxide", ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen", "Argon"], "Burning carbon fuels produces CO₂."),
     TF("The ozone hole is the main cause of global warming.", False, "They are separate problems; warming is caused mainly by greenhouse gases."),
     MT("Match each description to the term.", [("Gas released by rice fields and livestock", "Methane"), ("Main gas from burning fossil fuels", "Carbon dioxide"), ("Adjusting to impacts such as drought", "Adaptation"), ("Cutting emissions to limit warming", "Mitigation")], "Mitigation cuts emissions; adaptation copes with impacts; methane and CO₂ are the key gases."),
     M("Which agreement was signed in 2015?", "Paris Agreement", ["Kyoto Protocol", "Berlin Conference", "Treaty of Versailles"], "The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015."),
     O("Explain why Africa is considered vulnerable to climate change although its emissions are low.", "Many Africans depend on rain-fed agriculture, so changes in rainfall quickly affect food and income. Poverty and limited infrastructure reduce the ability to cope with floods, drought and heat. Weak health services and rapid population growth increase exposure.", "Dependence on farming (1); limited capacity (1); additional factor (1); link to emissions (1)."),
     O("Assess the effectiveness of mitigation and adaptation as responses to climate change in a developing country.", "Mitigation, like renewable energy and forest protection, helps in the long term only if all major emitters act; one country’s effect is small. Adaptation gives immediate protection, for example by improving water storage and drought-tolerant seeds, but it can be costly and does not remove the cause. Best results combine both, with funding and technology transfer.", "Mitigation assessed (2); adaptation assessed (2); conclusion (1).")],
    [("The enhanced greenhouse effect is caused mainly by", "Human release of extra greenhouse gases", ["The Moon", "Volcanoes only", "Ocean tides"], "Fossil fuels and deforestation add gases."),
     ("Methane is released by", "Livestock and rice fields", ["Solar panels", "Wind turbines", "Cold air"], "Both are sources."),
     ("Adaptation means", "Adjusting to changes that happen", ["Preventing all warming", "Ignoring risks", "Building more coal plants"], "It reduces vulnerability."),
     ("Sea-level rise threatens mainly", "Low-lying coasts and deltas", ["High mountains", "Deserts", "Plateaus"], "Coastal flooding."),
     ("Kyoto Protocol was adopted in", "1997", ["1884", "1960", "2020"], "Kyoto is from 1997.")],
    (cc_fig, "From greenhouse gas emissions to climate impacts.", "Four boxes joined by arrows from emissions to extra heat trapped and to impacts."),
    ["Dates of agreements: UNFCCC 1992, Kyoto 1997, Paris 2015 — verify.", "Impacts are stated in general terms; regional specifics (rain increase or decrease) differ and must be checked."])

des_fig = flow(["Drought and rising population", "Overgrazing, over-cultivation, wood cutting", "Vegetation lost, soil eroded", "Yields fall; people migrate"], 440, "orange", 13, 340)
L = lesson(ch, "desertification", "Desertification, the Sahel and Lake Chad",
    ["Define desertification and explain its causes and effects", "Describe the changes at Lake Chad", "Evaluate management strategies"],
    [("definition", "Desertification", "**Desertification** is land degradation in dry areas caused by climatic variation and human activity, turning productive land into desert-like conditions. It is not simply the spread of existing deserts. It is a concern in the **Sahel**, the semi-arid belt south of the Sahara, including the far north of Cameroon."),
     ("retenir", "Causes and effects", "**Causes:** recurring drought, overgrazing, over-cultivation without fallow, cutting wood for fuel, poor irrigation (salinisation) and population pressure. **Effects:** lower soil fertility and crop yields, loss of pasture, wind and water erosion, food shortages, migration and competition for resources."),
     ("retenir", "Lake Chad", "Lake Chad is shared by Cameroon, Chad, Niger and Nigeria. Since the 1960s it has shrunk greatly because of lower rainfall and increased abstraction of water for irrigation and population needs, though it varies year to year. Fishing, farming and herding around the lake, and many people’s livelihoods, are affected. The Lake Chad Basin Commission (created in 1964) coordinates the neighbouring countries."),
     ("methode", "Management", "Tree planting and shelterbelts, agroforestry, **zaï** pits and stone lines to hold water, improved cooking stoves, rotational grazing, small-scale irrigation, community land management, and regional projects such as the African Union’s **Great Green Wall** (launched in 2007). Success depends on local participation and secure land rights."),
     ("attention", "Common errors", "- Deserts do not simply ‘advance’ like a wave: degradation is local and caused by land use as well as climate.\n- Drought is a natural event; desertification needs human pressure too.\n- Do not give a precise area for Lake Chad without a source.")],
    [("Example 1 — Explain overgrazing", "Explain how overgrazing contributes to desertification.",
      ["Too many animals eat the grass faster than it regrows.", "Hooves compact the soil and bare ground is exposed.", "Wind and rain erode the topsoil.", "Soil fertility and water retention fall, so less grass can grow."], "A cycle of vegetation loss, erosion and falling fertility."),
     ("Example 2 — Lake Chad impacts", "Give two likely consequences for people when Lake Chad shrinks.",
      ["Fishing yields and fishing communities decline.", "Fertile recession farmland and pasture shift or reduce.", "Communities may migrate or compete for water and land."], "Loss of livelihoods and migration.")],
    [M("The Sahel is", "A semi-arid zone south of the Sahara", ["A tropical rainforest", "A polar region", "A mountain chain"], "Sahel means ‘shore’ of the desert."),
     TF("Desertification is only caused by natural drought.", False, "Human activity is also a major cause."),
     MT("Match each measure to its aim.", [("Shelterbelt", "Reduce wind erosion"), ("Zaï pits", "Capture rainwater for crops"), ("Improved stoves", "Cut demand for firewood"), ("Rotational grazing", "Allow pasture to recover")], "Each addresses a cause of degradation."),
     M("Which countries share Lake Chad (among others)?", "Cameroon, Chad, Niger and Nigeria", ["Gabon and Congo", "Kenya and Uganda", "Egypt and Libya"], "Four countries border the lake."),
     O("Explain why managing Lake Chad requires international cooperation.", "The lake and its basin are shared by several countries, so water taken upstream or by one state affects the others. Fish and people move across borders, and migration and pressure on resources are regional. A joint body can agree on water use and projects.", "Shared resource (1); effect of use in one country on others (1); mobility of people (1); joint body/solution (1)."),
     O("Discuss the causes of desertification in the Sahel and evaluate two strategies to combat it.", "Causes: drought, high livestock numbers, expanding cropland, fuelwood collection, rapid population growth. Strategies: tree planting and the Great Green Wall can restore cover and soils, but need long-term care and funding; zaï and stone lines are cheap and effective at farm level but need labour. Local participation and secure land rights improve success.", "Causes (2); two strategies (2); evaluation (1).")],
    [("Overcultivation causes", "Loss of soil fertility", ["More forests", "More rain", "Higher fertility"], "Without fallow, nutrients are depleted."),
     ("The Great Green Wall is", "A tree-planting and land restoration initiative across the Sahel", ["A concrete barrier", "A railway", "A dam"], "Launched by the African Union."),
     ("Salinisation is", "Build-up of salts in soil, often after poor irrigation", ["Soil erosion by wind", "A type of flood", "Forest fire"], "Evaporation leaves salts."),
     ("Lake Chad Basin Commission was created in", "1964", ["1884", "2015", "1920"], "It was set up in the 1960s."),
     ("Desertification is", "Land degradation in dry areas", ["Increase of rainfall", "Growth of forests", "A volcanic eruption"], "Definition.")],
    (des_fig, "A chain of causes and effects in desertification.", "Four boxes connected by arrows from drought and population growth to falling yields and migration."),
    ["Lake Chad shrinkage: confirm current scientific statements and avoid quoting area figures unless from a reliable source.", "Dates (Lake Chad Basin Commission 1964; Great Green Wall 2007) to be verified."])

def_fig = flow(["Clearing for farms, logging, fuelwood, roads", "Forest cover falls", "Soil erosion, lost habitats, less carbon storage", "Protection, sustainable use, replanting"], 440, "green", 13, 360)
L = lesson(ch, "deforestation", "Deforestation, biodiversity and conservation",
    ["Explain the causes and consequences of deforestation", "Describe the importance of the Congo Basin and Cameroon’s forests", "Evaluate conservation strategies"],
    [("definition", "Deforestation", "**Deforestation** is the permanent clearing of forest for other uses. In central Africa the main direct causes are **agricultural expansion** (shifting cultivation and plantations), **logging**, **fuelwood and charcoal**, road building, mining and urban growth. Underlying causes include population growth, poverty, demand for timber and weak enforcement."),
     ("retenir", "Consequences", "**Environmental:** soil erosion and flooding, loss of habitat and biodiversity, disturbed water cycle and local climate, release of stored carbon. **Social and economic:** loss of forest products (bushmeat, medicines, fruits, honey), loss of land for forest communities, but also short-term income from timber and farming."),
     ("retenir", "Cameroon and the Congo Basin", "Cameroon’s southern and eastern forests belong to the Congo Basin, one of the largest tropical rainforests in the world. Protected areas include national parks and reserves such as Korup and Dja. Strategies: sustainable and certified logging, protected areas, **community forestry**, agroforestry, reforestation, efficient stoves and international schemes that pay to keep forests standing."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- Logging is selective in many places; not all logging means total clearing.\n- Protected areas fail without involving local people and enforcement.\n- Planting a few trees does not replace the biodiversity of old forest.")],
    [("Example 1 — Impact chain", "Explain how clearing forest on a steep slope can cause flooding downstream.",
      ["Fewer trees mean less interception and less water taken up.", "Bare soil allows rapid run-off and erosion.", "Rivers receive water and sediment quickly and their channels silt up, so floods are more likely."], "Faster run-off and silted channels increase flood risk."),
     ("Example 2 — Conservation conflict", "A national park restricts hunting and farming at its boundary. Suggest how conflict with villagers could be reduced.",
      ["Involve communities in planning and management.", "Share benefits: jobs as guides, tourism revenue sharing.", "Support alternative livelihoods and agroforestry near the boundary."], "Participation, benefit sharing and alternative livelihoods.")],
    [M("Which is a direct cause of deforestation?", "Agricultural expansion", ["Higher rainfall", "Cloud cover", "Cooler nights"], "Clearing for farms is a major direct cause."),
     TF("Forests store carbon, so clearing them can release CO₂.", True, "Burning or decay of trees releases carbon."),
     MT("Match each strategy to its idea.", [("Community forestry", "Villagers manage forest use"), ("Reforestation", "Planting trees after clearing"), ("Protected area", "Legal restriction on land use"), ("Agroforestry", "Trees and crops grown together")], "Standard conservation concepts."),
     M("The Congo Basin forest is important because it", "Holds much biodiversity and stores carbon", ["Has no animals", "Is a desert", "Is all farmland"], "It is among the largest tropical forests."),
     O("Give three social or economic consequences of deforestation for forest communities.", "Loss of food, fuel, building materials and medicinal plants; loss of land and cultural sites; flooding and soil erosion hurting farms; fewer game animals; but some short-term income from timber and farm land.", "One mark per valid consequence, max 3."),
     O("Evaluate the effectiveness of protected areas and community forestry as ways of conserving tropical forests.", "Protected areas safeguard biodiversity and clear boundaries, but need funds, patrols and the acceptance of nearby people; they can restrict local livelihoods. Community forestry gives local people a stake and sustainable income, though it needs training, secure rights and markets. The best results combine both approaches with fair benefit sharing.", "Protected areas (2); community forestry (2); balanced conclusion (1).")],
    [("Fuelwood demand is", "A cause of forest loss", ["A conservation measure", "A cause of rain", "A volcano type"], "Cutting wood for cooking."),
     ("Korup is", "A protected rainforest area in Cameroon", ["A river in Chad", "A mine", "A port"], "Korup National Park is in the south-west."),
     ("Agroforestry combines", "Trees with crops or livestock", ["Rocks and sand", "Roads and bridges", "Mining and fishing"], "It protects soil and gives income."),
     ("Deforestation increases soil erosion because", "Roots and canopy no longer protect soil", ["Rain stops", "Soil gains nutrients", "Winds disappear"], "Bare soil is exposed."),
     ("Sustainable logging means", "Harvesting at a rate that allows forests to regrow", ["Cutting everything", "Burning forest", "Banning all use of wood"], "Balance of use and regeneration.")],
    (def_fig, "From clearing to conservation.", "Four boxes: causes of clearing, falling forest cover, consequences, and conservation responses."),
    ["Names of protected areas (Korup, Dja) — verify spelling and status.", "Congo Basin described qualitatively: add size or area only from a reliable source."])

