L = lesson(ch, "urban", "Urban geography: functions, land use and African cities",
    ["Describe the functions and hierarchy of settlements", "Explain urban land-use models and apply them to African cities", "Evaluate urban planning and sustainability"],
    [("definition", "Hierarchy and services", "Settlements form a **hierarchy**: many hamlets and villages, fewer towns, very few large cities. Services with a high **threshold** (minimum customers needed) and long **range** (distance people will travel) such as hospitals and universities occur in larger centres; corner shops occur everywhere. This is the basis of **central place theory**."),
     ("retenir", "Land-use models", "The **concentric zone** model has a Central Business District (CBD), an inner zone, then suburbs. The **sector** model shows wedges of land use along transport routes. African cities often depart from the models: colonial planning, a port or river site, traditional neighbourhoods and **informal settlements** on the margins or low land. Douala is the main port and economic city; Yaoundé is the political capital."),
     ("retenir", "Planning", "Challenges: housing, water, sanitation, transport, waste, floods, jobs. Responses: land-use planning and zoning, **site and service** schemes, slum upgrading with community involvement, mass transport, green spaces, drainage, and decentralised growth to secondary towns. A **sustainable city** meets present needs without harming future generations."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- Models are simplifications: real cities do not match them exactly.\n- Informal settlements are home to workers the city depends on; evictions without alternatives create new problems.\n- Capital city and economic capital are not always the same.")],
    [("Example 1 — Threshold and range", "Why is there a hospital in the regional town but only a clinic in a village?",
      ["A hospital needs a large population (high threshold) to stay open.", "People will travel a long way for a hospital (long range).", "A village does not have the threshold, so it has a clinic with a smaller catchment."], "Higher-order services need larger thresholds and are in larger centres."),
     ("Example 2 — Apply the model", "Describe how the concentric model may not fit a port city growing along a river.",
      ["Growth follows the river, port and main roads, not equal rings.", "Low or flood-prone land attracts informal housing.", "Industry stretches along the port and railway."], "Site, transport and informal growth distort the pattern.")],
    [M("In the concentric model the CBD is", "In the centre", ["At the edge", "Outside the city", "In a ring of farms"], "It is the heart of commerce."),
     TF("Higher-order services need a bigger threshold population.", True, "They need many customers to remain viable."),
     MT("Match each term to its meaning.", [("Threshold", "Minimum market needed for a service"), ("Range", "Distance people will travel"), ("CBD", "Core of shops and offices"), ("Informal settlement", "Housing built without formal planning")], "Standard urban terms."),
     M("Which is a sensible response to slums?", "Upgrade services with community involvement", ["Ignore them", "Demolish without alternatives", "Ban migration only"], "Improving water, drainage and tenure helps residents."),
     O("Explain two ways in which a city can reduce traffic congestion.", "Invest in public transport such as buses, so fewer private vehicles are needed. Improve road layout and traffic management, add pedestrian routes, and plan jobs and services near homes to shorten journeys.", "Two methods (2); explanation (2)."),
     O("Discuss the problems caused by rapid urban growth in African cities and evaluate possible solutions.", "Rapid growth brings housing shortage and informal settlements, poor water and sanitation, congestion, unemployment and floods. Solutions include slum upgrading, which is affordable and quick but needs land rights; planned housing and transport, which cost more; and developing smaller towns to ease pressure on large cities, which needs jobs and services there.", "Problems (2); solutions (2); evaluation (1).")],
    [("The top of the urban hierarchy has", "Few, large cities", ["Many hamlets", "Many villages", "No towns"], "Hierarchy narrows upward."),
     ("Range is", "The distance people will travel for a service", ["The price of a service", "The size of a town", "The number of shops"], "Definition."),
     ("A site and service scheme provides", "Plots with basic services for households to build on", ["Luxury hotels", "Free cars", "Factories only"], "A low-cost way to house people."),
     ("Informal sector work includes", "Small unregistered trade and services", ["Banking only", "Government offices", "Large factories only"], "Often casual."),
     ("A sustainable city", "Meets needs without harming future generations", ["Grows without limits", "Ignores pollution", "Has no transport"], "Sustainability definition.")],
    (zones_fig, "The concentric zone model of a city (simplified).", "Three concentric circles labelled CBD, inner zone and suburbs."),
    ["Statements about Douala and Yaoundé are limited to well-known roles; check any case-study details.", "Confirm which urban models the syllabus requires."])

ind_fig = boxes(["Raw materials", "Power and energy", "Market", "Transport", "Labour and skills", "Capital and policy"], 440, "blue", 13, 2, 38, 10)
L = lesson(ch, "industry-energy", "Industry and energy in Africa and Cameroon",
    ["Explain factors influencing the location of industry", "Describe the main types of industry in Cameroon", "Compare energy sources and their sustainability"],
    [("definition", "Industrial location", "Industries locate by balancing **raw materials, power, market, transport, labour, capital** and **government policy**. Agro-processing (cocoa, cotton, palm oil, timber), brewing, cement, aluminium, oil refining and small informal industries are common in Cameroon, with Douala as the main industrial centre because of its port, market and labour."),
     ("retenir", "Examples", "An aluminium smelter at Edéa uses hydroelectricity from the Sanaga river. An oil refinery operates at Limbe. Plantation agriculture around Mount Cameroon supplies processing plants. Challenges: unreliable power supply, high transport costs, small domestic markets, competition from imports, shortage of skilled workers and credit. Policies include industrial zones, incentives and regional trade."),
     ("retenir", "Energy", "**Non-renewable:** oil, gas, coal. **Renewable:** hydropower, solar, wind, biomass, geothermal. Hydroelectricity supplies a major part of Cameroon’s electricity but varies with rainfall; many households rely on **fuelwood and charcoal**, which adds to forest loss. Solar power has large potential in the sunny north. Rural electrification and efficient cooking are development priorities."),
     ("attention", "Do not say", "- That ‘renewable’ means ‘free of impact’: dams have social and environmental costs.\n- That industry always locates near raw materials: weight-gaining products locate near the market.\n- Use of precise energy statistics: use only qualitative statements unless you can cite a source.")],
    [("Example 1 — Location of an aluminium smelter", "Explain why an aluminium smelter may be located near a large hydroelectric power station.",
      ["Smelting uses very large amounts of electricity.", "Cheap, reliable power cuts costs.", "A port nearby helps import raw material and export the product."], "Electricity cost and port access."),
     ("Example 2 — Fuelwood and forests", "Explain why dependence on fuelwood is an environmental problem.",
      ["Many households cook with wood or charcoal.", "Cutting trees near towns causes deforestation and erosion.", "Smoke indoors harms health, so cleaner stoves and fuels help."], "Fuelwood use contributes to deforestation and health risks.")],
    [M("Which factor most strongly attracts industry to a large city?", "A big market and labour supply", ["Lack of roads", "No electricity", "Remote location"], "Cities have customers and workers."),
     TF("Hydropower is a renewable energy source.", True, "It uses the water cycle."),
     MT("Match each industry with an input it needs.", [("Aluminium smelting", "Large amounts of electricity"), ("Cocoa processing", "Cocoa beans"), ("Brewing", "Water and grain"), ("Cement", "Limestone")], "Primary inputs."),
     M("A weakness of dependence on hydropower is", "Output falls in dry periods", ["It uses oil", "It needs coal", "It produces CO₂ at the dam"], "River flow varies."),
     O("Explain two reasons why industrial development is limited in many African countries.", "Unreliable electricity raises costs and halts production. Small local markets and poor transport make selling difficult. Shortage of capital and skills limits expansion. Competition from cheaper imports hurts small firms.", "Two reasons (2); explanation (2)."),
     O("Discuss the opportunities and difficulties of developing renewable energy in Cameroon.", "Opportunities: large hydropower potential, plentiful sun in the north, biomass residues, and reduced need for imported fuel and forest cutting. Difficulties: high start-up cost, need for grid and transmission, seasonal river flow, local impacts of dams, and shortage of skilled technicians. Balance requires planning and finance.", "Opportunities (2); difficulties (2); balance (1).")],
    [("A big market in a city encourages", "Consumer goods industries", ["No industry", "Only farming", "Only fishing"], "Producers locate near customers."),
     ("Biomass energy comes from", "Wood and plant material", ["Uranium", "Coal only", "Wind"], "Organic matter."),
     ("Which causes forest loss in Africa?", "Fuelwood and charcoal demand", ["Solar panels", "Wind turbines", "Tidal energy"], "Cutting wood."),
     ("Industries that process farm products are called", "Agro-processing", ["Mining", "Fishing", "Printing"], "They add value to crops."),
     ("Renewable energy sources are", "Naturally replaced", ["Finite", "Only coal", "Only gas"], "They can be used repeatedly.")],
    (ind_fig, "Factors that influence the location of industry.", "Six boxes naming raw materials, power, market, transport, labour and capital and policy."),
    ["Details about Edéa, Limbe and the Sanaga are widely known but verify spelling and current operation.", "No production or energy statistics are given; add only from official sources."])

tot_fig = bars([("Export prices year 1", 100, "blue"), ("Import prices year 1", 100, "orange"), ("Export prices year 2", 80, "blue"), ("Import prices year 2", 120, "orange")], None, 440, 240)
L = lesson(ch, "trade", "Trade, development and regional integration",
    ["Use indicators to measure development", "Calculate and interpret terms of trade", "Describe aid, debt and regional trade blocs in Africa"],
    [("definition", "Development indicators", "Economic: **GDP per capita**. Social: life expectancy, literacy, infant mortality, access to clean water. The **Human Development Index (HDI)** combines income, health and education. Averages hide inequality inside countries; a mix of indicators gives the clearest picture."),
     ("formule", "Terms of trade", "$\\text{Terms of trade} = \\frac{\\text{export price index}}{\\text{import price index}} \\times 100$. A value below 100 means export prices have fallen relative to import prices: the country can buy fewer imports for the same exports.", "\\text{ToT} = \\frac{\\text{export index}}{\\text{import index}} \\times 100"),
     ("retenir", "Trade and cooperation", "Many African economies depend on **primary products** (oil, cocoa, coffee, timber, cotton, minerals) whose prices fluctuate, while manufactured imports cost more. **Aid** may be bilateral, multilateral or from NGOs; **debt** repayments can limit spending. Regional blocs aim to increase trade and cooperation: CEMAC (Central Africa, which includes Cameroon), ECOWAS (West Africa) and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA, agreed in 2018)."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid these errors", "- Do not equate GDP per capita with how well off everybody is.\n- A fall in terms of trade is NOT a fall in the quantity of exports.\n- Aid can help but may create dependency or be tied to conditions.")],
    [("Example 1 — Terms of trade", "Export price index = 80; import price index = 120. Calculate the terms of trade and interpret.",
      ["ToT = 80 ÷ 120 × 100.", "= 66.7 (1 d.p.).", "Below 100: the country can now buy only about two-thirds of the imports it could buy before for the same exports."], "About 66.7: worsening terms of trade."),
     ("Example 2 — Why diversify?", "Explain why dependence on one export crop is risky.",
      ["World prices change with supply and demand, so income is unstable.", "A pest or drought can destroy the crop.", "Diversification into processing and several products spreads the risk."], "Income instability and lack of resilience.")],
    [N("Export index 90, import index 120. Terms of trade?", 75, "90 ÷ 120 × 100 = 75.", 0.1),
     M("Which indicator combines income, health and education?", "HDI", ["GDP alone", "Birth rate", "Rainfall"], "The Human Development Index."),
     TF("A terms of trade value below 100 means exports can buy more imports than before.", False, "Below 100 means they can buy fewer."),
     M("CEMAC is a regional grouping in", "Central Africa", ["West Africa", "East Asia", "South America"], "It includes Cameroon."),
     MT("Match the term to its meaning.", [("Bilateral aid", "From one government to another"), ("Primary product", "Raw material or farm produce"), ("Diversification", "Producing many different goods"), ("Debt service", "Repayments of loans and interest")], "Standard terms."),
     O("Discuss whether foreign aid always helps a developing country.", "Aid can build schools, clinics and roads and respond to emergencies. But it may be tied to buying goods from donors, can create dependency, may be wasted through poor governance, and may not reach the poorest. Aid works best when it matches local priorities and builds skills. Trade and fair prices are also important.", "Benefits (2); problems (2); balanced judgement (1).")],
    [("Primary products are", "Raw materials and farm products", ["Computers", "Cars", "Machines"], "Little processing."),
     ("GDP per capita is", "Average income per person", ["Rainfall per year", "Deaths per 1000", "Total area"], "Total GDP divided by population."),
     ("AfCFTA aims to", "Create a continent-wide free trade area", ["Close borders", "Stop trade", "Create one language"], "African Continental Free Trade Area."),
     ("If imports become more expensive relative to exports", "Terms of trade worsen", ["Terms of trade improve", "Nothing changes", "Exports fall in kind"], "Fewer imports per unit of exports."),
     ("Multilateral aid comes from", "International organisations", ["Individual families", "A single firm", "A village"], "E.g. UN agencies and development banks.")],
    (tot_fig, "Illustrative price indices: year 1 equal, year 2 export prices fall and import prices rise.", "Four bars: export and import price indices for two years; the exports bar falls to 80 and imports rise to 120."),
    ["Bars are invented for teaching; no real index is implied.", "AfCFTA: agreed in 2018, trading began in 2021 — verify wording; check bloc membership lists."])

