L = lesson(ch, "cameroon", "Regional geography of Cameroon",
    ["Describe the main relief and climate regions of Cameroon", "Name the administrative regions and neighbours", "Relate the physical environment to land use and economic activity"],
    [("definition", "Position and regions", "Cameroon lies in Central and West Africa, at the head of the Gulf of Guinea, and is often called ‘Africa in miniature’ because it contains coast, forest, mountain, savanna and semi-desert. Neighbours: Nigeria, Chad, the Central African Republic, the Republic of the Congo, Gabon and Equatorial Guinea. It has **10 regions**: Adamawa, Centre, East, Far North, Littoral, North, North-West, South, South-West and West."),
     ("retenir", "Relief", "**Coastal plain** (Atlantic coast, mangroves, the Wouri estuary); **southern plateau** covered by equatorial forest; **western highlands** with volcanic mountains, including **Mount Cameroon** (over 4000 m, the highest peak in West and Central Africa); **Adamawa plateau** in the centre; **northern plains** of the Benue valley and the Lake Chad basin. Main rivers include the Sanaga, Wouri, Nyong and Benue."),
     ("retenir", "Climate and land use", "The south has hot, humid equatorial climate and forest, with cocoa, plantain and timber. The coast and western highlands are very wet, with plantations and, in the highlands, coffee and food crops. The Adamawa plateau is cooler savanna suited to cattle. The north is hotter and drier, with cotton, millet, sorghum and pastoralism."),
     ("pieges", "Be accurate", "- The country’s capital is Yaoundé (Centre); Douala (Littoral) is the largest city and main port.\n- Mount Cameroon is in the South-West region; Lake Chad borders the Far North.\n- Do not give precise areas or populations unless certain.")],
    [("Example 1 — Region and activity", "Match a farming system to the physical conditions of the western highlands.",
      ["Volcanic soils and cooler temperatures at altitude.", "Good rainfall and relief rain on slopes.", "Coffee, maize, vegetables and dense rural population."], "Coffee and mixed food farming on fertile volcanic soils."),
     ("Example 2 — North-south contrast", "Describe the change in climate from south to north and its effects on vegetation.",
      ["Rainfall decreases and the dry season lengthens northwards.", "Equatorial forest gives way to savanna, then to Sahelian thorn scrub.", "Farming shifts from cocoa and plantain to cotton, millet and herding."], "A drier, more seasonal climate and open savanna in the north.")],
    [M("Which region has Lake Chad on its border?", "Far North", ["South", "Littoral", "East"], "Lake Chad is in the extreme north."),
     TF("Yaoundé is the economic capital and main port.", False, "Douala is the main port and economic capital; Yaoundé is the political capital."),
     MT("Match each feature to its region.", [("Mount Cameroon", "South-West"), ("Wouri estuary and port", "Littoral"), ("Benue valley", "North"), ("Adamawa plateau", "Adamawa")], "General locations."),
     M("How many regions does Cameroon have?", "10", ["8", "12", "6"], "Ten administrative regions."),
     O("Explain why Cameroon is called ‘Africa in miniature’.", "It includes many of Africa’s environments in one country: coastal plains and mangroves, equatorial forest, volcanic mountains, savanna plateaux and semi-arid Sahel near Lake Chad. It also has great cultural and linguistic diversity.", "Environments (2); diversity (1); link to ‘miniature’ (1)."),
     O("Compare the physical geography and economic activities of the northern and southern regions of Cameroon.", "The south has a hot, humid equatorial climate with rainfall in most months, dense forest, red soils, and farming of cocoa, plantain and cassava, with timber. The north has a long dry season, savanna and Sahel vegetation, and one wet season; farming centres on cotton, millet, sorghum and cattle. Water supply and desertification are more critical in the north, while forest loss concerns the south.", "Climate/vegetation contrast (2); economic contrast (2); environmental issue (1).")],
    [("The highest peak in West and Central Africa is", "Mount Cameroon", ["Mount Kenya", "Kilimanjaro", "Mount Atlas"], "It is in the South-West region."),
     ("Which country does NOT border Cameroon?", "Kenya", ["Nigeria", "Chad", "Gabon"], "Kenya is in East Africa."),
     ("The Sanaga is", "A major river in Cameroon", ["A lake", "A mountain", "A desert"], "It flows to the Atlantic."),
     ("Cattle are especially important in", "Adamawa and the north", ["The coastal mangroves", "The ocean", "Urban centres only"], "Savanna pastures."),
     ("A coastal plain with mangroves is typical of", "The Littoral and South-West coast", ["The Far North", "Adamawa", "The East"], "Atlantic coastline.")],
    (cam_fig, "A south-north sequence of physical regions (schematic, south at the bottom).", "Four stacked boxes: Atlantic coast, southern plateau, Adamawa plateau and northern plains, from bottom to top."),
    ["Mount Cameroon height (just over 4000 m) and ‘highest in West and Central Africa’ to be verified.", "The 10-region list and neighbours are standard; confirm any boundary or naming changes."])

